Robin asks about "pancritical rationalism"-

> Folks have told me it comes down to "leave everything open to
> question, except of course for things you're absolutely sure of (like
> math proofs)".  But surely there must be more than this tautology.
> 
> Robin Hanson (M.S. Philosphy of Science, U. Chicago, '84)

He says stop requiring justification of things, and only pay attention
to what has survived criticism (on a couple of kinds of grounds).  
Popper extended.  PanCritRat is supposed to hold itself open to 
criticism, and hold proofs suspect, too (but stick with criticism based
on the ideas of logical consequences and inconsistency, a slightly
sticky point).

He says a weakness of rationalism has been its not distinguishing
criticism and justification well enough, so that calling something
unjustified was just considered one kind of criticism, without
noticing the fallback on accepted kinds of justification.

I don't get why the guy spent so much time discussing theology.
He says it's just an example, but...

