From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Date: Tue, 25 Jan 94 00:25:21 GMT
Subject: [#94-1-480] The Go-Go Future         

I am grateful for Duncan casting doubt on the claims that wages have
declined, although I'd like to see figures for the missing components
(state and self-employment) he mentioned.  Like Duncan, though, I am
wary of "productivity" figures.  I prefer to stick with per capita real
GDP figures - they relate to how rich we are as individuals, correct for
population growth, emigrations, inflation etc.

I have some good news for Duncan.  Talking about economic growth 
he says:

> Economic growth has slowed.

But has it?  Viewed historically it has increased.  Real per capita
growth has tripled or quadrupled over the last century, in the West, the
post WW-II being a golden era of growth.  Conventional (or popular)
wisdom (if that isn't oxymoronic) says that growth rates have dipped
from the 1950s as we entered the 1970s (oil crash) and never recovered
(fully) since.

I have data [*] that refutes this, for the USA and the UK, from _The
Economist_.  From an International Finance survey a couple of years
back:

Per capita real annualised GDP growth for:
              UK     USA      Japan      W.Germany      France
1950-90      2.2%    1.9%       6%         3.3%          3.3%
1980-90      2.3%    1.85       3.8%       1.35%         1.75%

There is no evidence of a slow down in either the US or the UK.  Japan,
having started industrialisation late, is dropping back from a break-neck
rebound from WW-II ruins (which Continential Europe had as well to boost
the long range figures) to merely fast.  Perhaps W.Germany's figures are
damped down by reunification costs?  (Can't remember when the Berlin
Wall fell down.  Was it 1989?)  As for France - they're all mad
socialists there - serves 'em right!

But in the US and UK.  No slow down.

Mike Price                              price@price.demon.co.uk
                                        Anarchy-PPL client

[*]  Next to the per capita data is a graph showing _unadjusted_ real
GDP.  It shows the usual "decline" in growth rates in all countries
except the UK (whose population is rather more stable than the others).

