From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Tue, 25 Jan 94 02:30:42 GMT
Subject: [#94-1-483] Extropianism is *not* a religion.

Mike Price comments on the Principles v.2.5 (July 1993):
>> Faith in a better future is confidence that an external force, 
>> whether God, State, or extraterrestrials, will solve our problems.
>
>This notion that faith is somehow irrational is discussed (and trounced)
>in _Atheism: the case against God_ by George(?) Smith.

In the preceding sentence in the Principles, the faith is defined as
passive: "Dynamic optimism and passive faith are incompatible."  So it's
"submissive" or "inactive" faith.  And in the previous section there are
some references to "blind faith". 

Here's two definitions of faith from my dictionary:

faith  n. 1. trust  2. belief (without proof)

Max is using def. (2) and Mike is using def. (1).  I think the context
suggests that definition (2) is the likely intention, but in case there's
any ambiguity perhaps the adjective "blind" or "uncritical" should be
attached to every instance of "faith".

Mike then points out how faith is not necessarily irrational -- using it
in its "trust" interpretation, if critical then it's perfectly rational.  

>The entire modern scientific process is based on faith.  I have
>_critical_ faith in stuff I read in refereed journals.  A "good" journal
>establishes a reputation in the scientific market-place by carefully
>validating articles with reputable reviewers and attracting good quality
>work.  Scientists build up their reputations by publishing in respected
>journals.  The loop is closed.  The whole process relies on the rational
>use of faith.

Yes, I decided to draw a diagram demonstrating both types of faith:

                                                                _________
                                           __________          |         |
                                          |          |   no    | alleg-  |
                                          | prophets |----<----| ations  |
                                          |__________|  proof  |_________|
 ________             __________   Passive      | 
|        |    no     |          |_______________| faith=trust & belief w/o 
| brain  |___________| external | {both no proof}                    proof
|        |   proof   |  world   |_______________ faith=trust
|________|           |__________|               |               _________
                                   Dynamic _____|______        |         |
                                   (or is |            | proof | likely  |
                                     it?) | scientists |---<---| facts   |
                                          |____________|       |_________| 
                                                        Dynamic


A major difference is that those who believe the Fairy Godmother, or
tongues of fire, or somesuch will save them are using the branch where
there is no proof anywhere along the line.  Maybe the allegation is
unfalsifiable, and in any case they're going to blindly trust the dogma
without allowing any criticism.  On the other hand, the scientific
theories must withstand the rigors of the scientific marketplace.

Even in the case of critical faith, there has to be some lack of proof
somewhere along the line.  For the scientists, you can short circuit  
external world - scientists, but there is still the possibility of being
a brain in a vat or living in a simulated universe.  At least, we can
admit this, assume it's probably not true, then proceed to the scientific
/ mathematical theories.  With probably a little more uncertainty 
introduced due to induction or measurement limitations.  The total 
uncertainty is still far lower than that of uncritical faith.

Another difference is that the conclusions of uncritical faith often
contravene scientific or natural law.  Supporters of dogma may counter
this by claiming that science contravenes religious law, but their laws
haven't had to withstand the rigors of critical analysis and satisfy
every test of empiricism and logic that can be put to them.  So I don't
believe in anything which is prohibited by natural law, e.g. "God".  In
fact, belief in the supernatural would be incompatible with Dynamic
Optimism.

